    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A TWIA claim in Port Arthur works differently from a claim with a regular homeowners insurer. The Texas Windstorm Insurance Association is the insurer of last resort for wind and hail along the Texas coast, and Jefferson County is one of the first-tier coastal counties it serves, according to the <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/wind/generalquestio.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Department of Insurance</a>. Many Port Arthur homes carry a homeowners policy that excludes wind and hail, a TWIA policy for those perils, and a separate flood policy. When a hurricane or strong thunderstorm hits, the TWIA policy is often the one that pays for the roof.</p>
<p>TWIA claims are governed by Subchapter L-1 of <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.2210.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Insurance Code Chapter 2210</a>, which sets its own timelines and restricts how disputes are handled. Knowing those rules early can keep a valid claim from slipping past a deadline.</p>

<h2>Key TWIA Claim Deadlines</h2>
<p>The figures below come from TWIA’s own <a href="https://www.twia.org/claims/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">claims page</a> and Chapter 2210. Deadlines can be extended in some catastrophes, and your policy and notice letters control. Always read the dates printed on each letter TWIA sends.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>General timeline</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Report the claim</td><td>Within one year from the date the property was damaged</td></tr>
<tr><td>TWIA requests information</td><td>Generally within 30 days after the claim is filed</td></tr>
<tr><td>TWIA accepts or rejects the claim</td><td>Generally within 60 days after receiving the requested information (extensions are possible)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Payment after acceptance</td><td>TWIA states it has 10 days to pay once coverage is accepted and requested materials are received</td></tr>
<tr><td>Request appraisal on the amount (ACV)</td><td>60 days from receipt of the notice letter; more time for replacement cost items</td></tr>
<tr><td>Notify TWIA of a coverage dispute</td><td>Within two years after receiving the claim decision</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Compare that to a typical homeowners insurer, which falls under the Prompt Payment of Claims rules in Chapter 542. Owners who have both policies after a hurricane will be running two different clocks at the same time.</p>

<h2>What TWIA Covers, and What It Does Not</h2>
<p>A TWIA policy covers direct physical loss from windstorm and hail, subject to its deductibles and terms. In Port Arthur that commonly includes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Shingles, decking, and underlayment torn or lifted by wind</li>
<li>Rain damage inside the home when wind or hail first created an opening in the roof or walls</li>
<li>Siding, soffit, fascia, gutters, and windows damaged by wind or wind-borne debris</li>
<li>Fences and other structures, depending on the policy</li>
<li>Hail damage to roofing and exterior materials</li>
</ul>
<p>TWIA does not cover flood, storm surge, or rising water. After a storm like Ike, when surge pushed into Sabine Pass and low-lying parts of the county, many claims split between TWIA for wind damage above the water line and a flood insurer for damage below it. Our <a href="/services/hurricane-damage-claims/">hurricane damage claims</a> page explains how those claims fit together.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/photos/public-adjuster-roof-inspection-tarp.webp" alt="Public adjuster inspecting a tarped roof after windstorm damage" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Temporary tarping protects the home and is usually part of the claim. Photograph the damage before the tarp goes on.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>The WPI-8 and Windstorm Inspection Requirements</h2>
<p>To be eligible for TWIA coverage, structures, additions, and repairs in the catastrophe area, including re-roofs and roof repairs, must be built and inspected to TDI’s windstorm specifications. TDI issues a WPI-8 Certificate of Compliance after a passing inspection (<a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/wind/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TDI Windstorm Inspection Program</a>). The City of Port Arthur also requires permits for re-roofing and structural repairs under the 2021 International Residential and Building Codes.</p>
<p>This affects your claim in two ways. First, your contractor needs to know about the windstorm inspection before work starts. Second, the cost of meeting those requirements may or may not be part of the claim payment, depending on your coverage and how the estimate is written. Our <a href="/local-building-codes/">Port Arthur building codes</a> page covers the local permit side.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>Where TWIA Claims Often Run Into Trouble</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Late reporting.</strong> Slow leaks after a storm get reported months later, and the cause becomes harder to prove.</li>
<li><strong>Wind vs. flood.</strong> Interior damage gets attributed to rising water rather than roof openings.</li>
<li><strong>Partial roof scope.</strong> TWIA’s estimate repairs a few slopes when the damage, matching, or code issues support more.</li>
<li><strong>Prior damage.</strong> Wear, older storms, or earlier unrepaired claims get blamed for current damage.</li>
<li><strong>Missed appraisal window.</strong> An owner disagrees with the amount but does not respond within the deadline on the notice letter.</li>
</ul>
<p>TWIA also allows supplemental claims when additional damage is found during repairs. Our <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">insurance claim supplements</a> page explains how those are usually documented. For disputes over the amount, see <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a>.</p>

<h2>How a Port Arthur Public Adjuster Can Help With TWIA</h2>
<p>A public adjuster represents you, not TWIA. On a TWIA claim that may include reviewing your TWIA policy and declarations, inspecting and measuring the roof and exterior, preparing a detailed Xactimate estimate, separating wind damage from flood damage when both are present, tracking each TWIA deadline, and responding to information requests. If you are not sure whether your claim is still timely, reach out early. Port Arthur Public Adjuster is a service of [[company]], TDI license #[[license]].</p>
<p>A public adjuster is not a lawyer. If TWIA has denied coverage and you are considering a lawsuit, Chapter 2210 has special notice and dispute rules, and you should speak with a Texas attorney. For a walkthrough of the full process, read our <a href="/twia-windstorm-claim-guide-port-arthur/">TWIA windstorm claim guide for Port Arthur</a>.</p>

<h2>TWIA Coverage Across Jefferson County</h2>
<p>TWIA serves property owners throughout Jefferson County, including Port Arthur, <a href="/service-areas/nederland/">Nederland</a>, <a href="/service-areas/port-neches/">Port Neches</a>, Groves, Sabine Pass, and Beaumont. Orange County is not in the TWIA catastrophe area, so owners in Orange and Bridge City usually get wind coverage through their homeowners carrier or the Texas FAIR Plan instead.</p>
